In article <HAIRSTON.90Jun11234039@henry.ece.cmu.edu> hairston@henry.ece.cmu.edu (David Hairston) writes:
:[dynamics@janus.Berkeley.EDU (Dynamics Group) writes:]
:[] Well I wasn't going to enter into this madness, but...
:...
:[] Perhaps some kind of prompt could be added, like a >
:[] in the upper left hand corner to signal "Yes I'm waiting for you to hit
:[] a key"?
:
:hey, i like this: a prompt somewhere to indicate that the game is idling
:for input (as opposed to doing some processing). that _would_ be useful
:on crowded levels like the castle.
     Thirded.  And if it's processing, display the command currently
being processed.  Thus if I hit h (or 4), it would display "left" until
it finished moving me left.  If I hit "d", it would display "drop"
until I finished dropping the object and it finished doing all the
necessary processing to get me back to the plain command line. 
Obviously, sometimes this would just be flashes.  This is likely to slow
down games at low baud rates.  Perhaps it should be an option.
     It would be most useful while running, of course.  "Run left"  Or
resting ("wait for 400", for instance, while other repeat commands might
display "search for 5").  If the command is stopped for some reason, the
status line should probably say, for example, "You stop waiting after
223 turns." rather than "You stop waiting."  This comes in handy when
I'm waiting to become weak but don't want to overshot.  The > prompt
would also come in handy while waiting for shortish periods, since ^A is
used both to stop waiting and to repeat previous commands (and I'm
constantly doing the wrong thing, because there's no easy indication
that the command is finished.  The cursor moves, but the cursor can
under some circumstances be anywhere on the screen, and if the game is
moving quickly it may be necessary to hit ^A right away without taking
time to check).
     Anyway, it shouldn't slow down anything but the beginning and end of a
command, since there would be no updates during execution.

:[ghudson@cie.uoregon.edu (Greg Hudson) writes:]
:[] Asking a player for confirmation or such after drinking the potion
:[] isn't a problem if you ask me: it's already done with levitation.
:
:to me, you should levitate for an _extended_ period when quaffing a blessed
:potion of levitation. it would give you an oppurtunity to use your adorable
:amulet of elevation (i.e. while levitating, donning the amulet forces you
:down. a cursed amulet might resemble a loadstone, a blessed one might allow
:you to levitate if you weren't already!)
     I think you're making the unstated assumption that blessed potions
are always more effective rather than always more (or at least equally)
helpful than an uncursed potion.  On the contrary; what you describe fits
better as a *cursed* potion of levitation.  Sure, it's better *if* you
have an amulet of elevation, but that's still not a blessing; that's a
conditional blessing.  (Just as a cursed scroll of teleportation is
actually a blessing if you have teleport control.  It's still an
undesirable effect if you don't assume anything.)

